1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,847
Welcome to slideSonnet.

2
00:00:01,847 --> 00:00:06,960
Everything you're about to hear was written as plain text in a sidecar file,

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:09,180
and rendered straight from a PDF.

4
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:10,525
Let me show you how.

5
00:00:11,325 --> 00:00:14,275
So what does slideSonnet actually do?

6
00:00:15,175 --> 00:00:17,846
It compiles narration from plain text.

7
00:00:17,846 --> 00:00:22,555
You write what each slide should say, a synthesized voice reads it,

8
00:00:22,555 --> 00:00:26,350
and your PDF becomes a timed, subtitled lecture video.

9
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:31,025
Why a computer voice? I could record myself.

10
00:00:31,925 --> 00:00:37,285
Because slides change, and every change means re-recording. Text doesn't mind.

11
00:00:37,285 --> 00:00:39,621
Fix the sentence, re-render, done.

12
00:00:39,621 --> 00:00:44,225
Your video's source becomes git-diffable text, versioned like code.

13
00:00:45,125 --> 00:00:46,903
Step one: mark your slides.

14
00:00:46,903 --> 00:00:50,195
Add the slideSonnet package to your Beamer source,

15
00:00:50,195 --> 00:00:54,409
and give every slide a stable id with the ess-ess-eye-dee macro.

16
00:00:54,409 --> 00:00:59,347
The id gets stamped invisibly onto each page, so narration binds to the id,

17
00:00:59,347 --> 00:01:03,100
not to a page number that shifts when you insert a slide.

18
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,192
Step two: scaffold the sidecar.

19
00:01:06,192 --> 00:01:09,020
Run slidesonnet init on your PDF, and it

20
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:11,989
reads the ids straight out of the document

21
00:01:11,989 --> 00:01:14,675
and writes a blank narration file, one

22
00:01:14,675 --> 00:01:17,645
block per slide, ready for you to fill in.

23
00:01:17,645 --> 00:01:19,200
By hand, or by an LLM.

24
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:24,025
Each slide id gets a block of utterances and pauses.

25
00:01:24,025 --> 00:01:27,300
Wait. Is that the block we're hearing right now?

26
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,575
Yes. Word for word.

27
00:01:30,475 --> 00:01:33,975
Do I really have to write that text file by hand?

28
00:01:34,875 --> 00:01:38,750
You can, or you can run slidesonnet edit for a local editor.

29
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:42,367
Page through the deck, type narration beside each slide,

30
00:01:42,367 --> 00:01:44,175
and preview the whole thing.

31
00:01:44,175 --> 00:01:46,306
The preview bakes in your pauses,

32
00:01:46,306 --> 00:01:49,600
so it plays back exactly like the final video will.

33
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:51,785
Step four: render.

34
00:01:51,785 --> 00:01:57,282
Run slidesonnet export with the kokoro engine for free, natural local speech,

35
00:01:57,282 --> 00:01:59,852
or a cloud engine for studio voices.

36
00:01:59,852 --> 00:02:01,422
Want a fast rough cut?

37
00:02:01,422 --> 00:02:05,777
Use silent mode, or estimate timing, to skip speech entirely.

38
00:02:05,777 --> 00:02:09,775
Subtitles are written alongside the video automatically.

39
00:02:10,675 --> 00:02:13,850
Doesn't re-rendering everything get slow?

40
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:16,659
Audio is cached by content.

41
00:02:16,659 --> 00:02:22,314
Edit one block, and only that line is re-synthesized. Everything else is reused.

42
00:02:22,314 --> 00:02:27,050
And switching engines never throws away audio you already paid for.

43
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:29,694
That's the whole workflow.

44
00:02:29,694 --> 00:02:34,323
Mark your slides, scaffold the narration, check that the ids line up,

45
00:02:34,323 --> 00:02:36,403
write and preview, then export.

46
00:02:36,403 --> 00:02:40,361
Every step is scriptable, so an LLM or a Makefile can drive

47
00:02:40,361 --> 00:02:43,850
the entire pipeline without ever opening the editor.

48
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:49,575
Mark your slides. Write narration. Render. Give slideSonnet a try.
